= Unit 3 · Simple types and judgments

== Lecture 5 · Typing as an inductive judgment
#focus[Build typing derivations, distinguish terms from judgments, and prove weakening for the simply typed calculus.]

*Recall and approach.* Recall implication introduction discharges an assumption and application requires a function and a matching argument type.


The simply typed lambda calculus assigns a type to each term according to rules. Our normalization core has types generated by a base type $o$ and arrows $A -> B$, with arrows associating to the right. We may include one constant $c:o$; it has no reduction rule. There is no unrestricted fixed-point operator.

#definition[Context and typing][A context $Gamma$ is a finite list of distinct variables with simple types. The judgment $Gamma tack.r t:A$ is generated by the variable rule, the constant rule, abstraction, and application. It asserts that $t$ has type $A$ under the assumptions in $Gamma$.]

#inference([$Gamma "," x:A tack.r t:B$], [$Gamma tack.r(lambda x.t):A -> B$], name: "Arrow introduction")
#inference([$Gamma tack.r f:A -> B quad Gamma tack.r u:A$], [$Gamma tack.r f u:B$], name: "Arrow elimination")

In the variable rule, if $x:A$ occurs in $Gamma$, then $Gamma tack.r x:A$. The constant has type $o$ in every valid context. A derivation is a finite tree of rule applications, and induction on that tree is the appropriate proof method for most typing lemmas.

#example[Composition][Under $f:B -> C,g:A -> B,x:A$, application first gives $g x:B$ and then $f(g x):C$. Abstract successively over $x,g,f$ to obtain the closed term $lambda f.lambda g.lambda x.f(g x)$ of type $(B -> C)->(A -> B)->A -> C$.]

#strategy[Induct on typing derivations; each constructor is handled by rebuilding the same rule from its transformed premises.]

#theorem[Weakening][If $Gamma tack.r t:A$ and $Gamma'$ extends $Gamma$ with fresh assumptions without changing existing assignments, then $Gamma' tack.r t:A$.]
#proof[Induct on the typing derivation. A variable still has its original assignment, and the constant rule remains available. The application case follows from the two induction hypotheses. For abstraction, rename the bound variable fresh for the enlarged context, apply the induction hypothesis to the body derivation, and reapply abstraction.]

#theorem[Typable terms use only declared variables][If $Gamma tack.r t:A$, then $FV(t)$ is contained in the variables declared by $Gamma$.]
#proof[Induct on the derivation. Variables and constants satisfy the claim. Application takes a union of free-variable sets. Abstraction removes its bound variable, which is exactly the additional assumption discharged by that rule.]

#warning[In the Curry-style syntax used here, an unannotated term may admit many simple types; for instance the identity has $A -> A$ for any $A$. A fixed derivation specifies a type, but a general uniqueness-of-type claim would require an annotated syntax or additional qualifications.]
#example[An explicit derivation tree for repeated application][Under $f:A->A,x:A$, variable rules give both declarations. Application derives $f x:A$, then a second application derives $f(f x):A$. Abstracting over $x$ yields $f:A->A tack.r lambda x.f(f x):A->A$; abstracting over $f$ gives $(A->A)->A->A$.]

The repeated occurrence of $f$ must use the same declaration each time. This is where typing becomes a constraint on syntax rather than a label attached independently to each occurrence. If the first application returned a different type, it could not automatically be fed back into $f$.

#checkpoint("3.1", [Why can $lambda x.x x$ not be typed with finite simple types?], [The same $x$ would need type $A$ as an argument and $A -> B$ as a function, forcing $A=A -> B$, impossible for finite type expressions.])
#takeaways[Types are syntax; typing is a judgment justified by rules; a derivation is evidence for that judgment. Keep these levels separate.]
